INT. DROWNED CATHEDRAL - NAVE - TWILIGHT

Seawater fills the cathedral to the height of a man's knee, black and perfectly still. Through the shattered rose window a violet sky bleeds in. Pews rise from the flood like the ribs of some enormous animal, and pale lantern-fish drift between them, trailing threads of cold light.

A flat-bottomed boat noses between the pillars. In the stern, a woman poles steadily, her coat the green of old copper. In the bow sits a man with a ledger across his knees, and between them, a girl hugs a birdcage covered in cloth.

                    NERISSA
(calling ahead)
Mind the altar rail. It bites.

                    BARNABY
It does not bite. It is a rail.

                    NERISSA
It took my last pole in half.

                    BARNABY
Because you steered it into the rail.

The girl has not spoken. She watches the water as though something might rise out of it. Under the cloth, something in the cage shifts and murmurs.

                    ODALYS
It's awake.

                    NERISSA
It's not awake.

                    BARNABY
It is certainly awake.

                    ODALYS
It wants out.

                    NERISSA
They always want out, love. That's the nature of--

                    BARNABY
We agreed that it stays covered until the choir loft.

                    NERISSA
We agreed nothing. You agreed. I poled.

He closes the ledger with a snap. She leans harder into the pole. The boat glides through a drift of drowned hymn-books, their pages spread like pale hands.

At the far end of the nave, the water begins to move. A slow ring spreads from the base of the pulpit, then another. The lantern-fish scatter.

Odalys sees it first. Her grip tightens on the cage. She says nothing.

                    BARNABY
Nerissa.

                    NERISSA
I see it.

                    BARNABY
You are not looking at it.

                    NERISSA
I am looking at everything. That is the point of looking.

                    ODALYS
It's under us.

The pole stops mid-stroke. Water drips off the end and sets small circles ringing outward. Under the boat, something enormous and unhurried turns over in the dark.

                    BARNABY
(very softly)
Give it the bird.

                    ODALYS
No.

                    NERISSA
Odalys--

                    ODALYS
It's mine. It sings for me.

                    BARNABY
Child, I am asking you--

                    NERISSA
Don't ask her, Barnaby, you'll only make it worse--

                    BARNABY
I am being perfectly reasonable!

                    NERISSA
You are shouting in a church!

The boat lifts. Not much. Enough that the water slides off the hull in a sheet. Odalys pulls the cloth from the cage in a single sweep.

Inside, a small gray wren opens its beak and sings a single, impossible note. Every pane left in the rose window answers it.

The water goes still.

Barnaby lowers the ledger. Nerissa lowers the pole. Odalys, eyes shut, holds the cage up toward the light.

Slowly, the boat settles.
